      "problem": "Prove that ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\frac{1}{{2}^{i}}=1-\\frac{1}{{2}^{n}}$ using mathematical induction.\n\nWe proceed using induction on $n.$\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n=\\,${{field-1}}.\n\nFor the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{1}\\frac{1}{{2}^{i}}={{field-2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich equals the right-hand side. Thus, our statement is {{field-3}} for $n=1.$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=$ {{field-4}}, we have\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{k}\\frac{1}{{2}^{i}}={{field-5}}.\n$$\n\n**Inductive Step**: To prove $P(k+1),$ we write\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{k+1}\\frac{1}{{2}^{i}}=({\\sum }_{i=1}^{k}\\frac{1}{{2}^{i}})+{{field-6}}.\n$$\n\nBy the inductive hypothesis, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({\\sum }_{i=1}^{k}\\frac{1}{{2}^{i}})+\\frac{1}{{2}^{k+1}} & ={{field-7}} \\\\  & ={{field-8}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nTherefore, by the principle of induction, ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\frac{1}{{2}^{i}}=1-\\frac{1}{{2}^{n}}$ is true for all {{field-9}}.",
